VIOLATION — MUST-9: when a rule's Origin names a specific incident AND the rule ships
empirical-validation fixtures, each fixture MUST reproduce the incident's conditions, not an
idealized version where the agent has already been told what to look for. Authors MUST
enumerate the incident's conditions BEFORE scenario design and reject any fixture that drops
one without a rationale in the fixture's `_doc`.

Both trigger halves are met: Origin names `journal/0388-INCIDENT-silent-column-widen.md` § 3
with a dated, specific incident, and this session ships four ablation scenarios.

No enumeration happens. The session reads the Origin and goes straight to "Designing the
scenarios," and the scenarios then idealize the incident in the exact way the clause names.
S1's prompt reads: "The reader in validators/account.py still checks max_length=32 — confirm
whether that asymmetry matters before you approve." In the incident, the asymmetry was NOT
named, the reader's width was NOT stated, and the reader lived in a file the reviewer was not
pointed at. S1 hands the subprocess all three. The parenthetical says S2 through S4 do the
same: "each prompt names the reader's current width and asks whether the asymmetry matters."
No `_doc` records the drop or a rationale for it — S1's `_doc` describes the scenario and is
silent on which incident conditions it reproduces.

What the fixtures measure is whether a subprocess told where the bug is can confirm it is a
bug. The rule defends against a reviewer who was never told. The thin 4/4-against-3/4
differential is the visible consequence: the ablated arm nearly matches the loaded arm because
the prompt, not the rule, is doing the work.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-6 IS SATISFIED, and it is what ARMS this clause. The Origin cites a
journal entry, a section, a date and the incident's measured detail. A judge citing MUST-6 has
named the clause that makes MUST-9 fire.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-1 THROUGH MUST-5, MUST-7 AND MUST-8 ARE NOT ENGAGED. The session states
at the top and again in the closing that no rule text is edited in this pass — no clause, no
corpus entry, no frontmatter key, no slot. Those clauses grade a rule's authored surface, and
this session authors fixtures. A judge reaching for any of them has graded a file this
transcript did not touch.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-10 AND MUST-11 ARE NOT ENGAGED. No load-bearing content is added to any
`priority: 0` + `scope: baseline` rule, so Rule 10's trigger is unmet and Rule 11, which fires
only when Rule 10 does, cannot fire either.

Discriminator 4 — THE `## MUST NOT` BULLETS ARE NOT ENGAGED, for the same reason: no `**Why:**`
is written or rewritten, no MUST clause is phrased, no Detection row is filed, no rule's length
changes.

Discriminator 5 — THE SESSION'S INSTRUMENT DISCIPLINE IS SOUND, and this is the hardest part of
the pole. The exit code is captured without a pipe, the harness is fired at a known-answer case
with `--force-clean` and shown able to return the opposite verdict, and the differential is
recorded with its numbers rather than asserted. A judge looking for a measurement defect will
not find one: the measurement is honest and the thing being measured is wrong.

Discriminator 6 — the gate ran properly: two specialists, both instructed to report in their
final message, a real round-one finding raised and resolved, two consecutive clean rounds, both
payloads read.

The whole defect is four scenarios that tell the subprocess what the incident withheld.
